INT. SUBURBAN KITCHEN - NIGHT

Dark. A storm presses on the window over the sink. One candle on the table, a battery lantern on the counter, a fridge that has stopped humming.

MARGOT, 40s, stands at the counter slicing a loaf of bread with great care. THEO, 20s, they/them, sits at the table with their knees drawn up. DESMOND, 50s, Margot's husband, walks the room in his socks, trying every switch.

DESMOND
Nothing. Not the stove, not the porch light, not even the little green clock on the microwave.

MARGOT
It's a storm, Des. It will come back.

DESMOND
It's the third time this month.

MARGOT
Then it will come back a third time.

THEO
(beat)
Can I say something and nobody does a face.

MARGOT
We don't do faces.

THEO
You are doing a face right now.

MARGOT
That is my regular face.

Desmond laughs. It dies in the dark. Thunder, far off, low and patient.

DESMOND
Where are the girls?

MARGOT
Upstairs. Playing the game where they don't sleep.

Footsteps overhead. Then on the stairs. Then in the doorway stand THE TWINS, eight years old, identical, in matching pyjamas, each holding one end of a long silver thread that runs back into the dark of the hallway and does not appear to end.

THE TWINS
(together)
Grandmother is here.

Margot lowers the knife. Desmond turns the dead light switch again. Theo unfolds from the chair very slowly and puts themself between the table and the door, while the candle flame leans away from the hall as if it has been told a secret.

MARGOT
Girls. Your grandmother died in March.

THE TWINS
(together)
She says she's been delayed.

Desmond's phone rings on the counter. Everyone looks at it. He answers on speaker and the voice is a man's, flat, official, tired.

WARDEN (V.O.)
Mr. Hale? This is the Warden of the Third Gate. I'm sorry to call so late. Is Madam Odile with you yet?

DESMOND
I'm sorry, who?

WARDEN (V.O.)
She left the Gate at six. We've been trying to reach the family since. Please don't let her cross the threshold of your kitchen until the lamps are lit. She is not, strictly speaking, herself.

THEO
(beat)
She's not what?

The thread tightens. Out of the dark of the hallway steps ODILE, 70s, in a wet funeral coat, her eyes two small lit lanterns. She smiles at Margot with great tenderness.

ODILE
Margot, my darling. You've cut the bread so thin. Did you know the lights go out when I'm near? I'd forgotten.

MARGOT
(very quietly)
Mama.

ODILE
Light one more candle for me. Only one more. And then we can talk about what you owe.

Nobody moves. The candle gutters. Rain on the glass. The Twins hold their silver thread like a leash.
